![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

To ensure the system fails closed gracefully, file reading and JSON parsing operations should be wrapped in a try-catch block. This prevents unhandled exceptions from crashing the validation process if any of the upstream configuration or artifact files are missing or malformed.

```suggestion
function readJson(root, relPath) {
  try {
    const fullPath = resolvePath(root, relPath);
    return JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(fullPath, 'utf8'));
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Diagnostic: Failed to read or parse JSON file at ' + relPath + ':', error.message);
    return null;
  }
}
```

<details>
<summary>References</summary>

1. When reading and parsing JSON input files, wrap the path resolution and file reading logic to catch errors and record diagnostics rather than throwing unhandled exceptions, ensuring the system fails closed gracefully.
</details>

![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

Calculating file hashes should be wrapped in a try-catch block to handle I/O failures or unreadable files gracefully, returning null to preserve fail-closed behavior.

```suggestion
function sha256File(root, relPath) {
  try {
    return crypto.createHash('sha256').update(fs.readFileSync(resolvePath(root, relPath))).digest('hex');
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Diagnostic: Failed to calculate hash for file at ' + relPath + ':', error.message);
    return null;
  }
}
```

<details>
<summary>References</summary>

1. When reading files or calculating file hashes, wrap the operations in a defensive try-catch block to handle unreadable files or I/O failures gracefully, returning null to preserve fail-closed behavior.
</details>

